 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "You live in Japan, where the ground shakes, so you build a solid house on footings. Problem solved!",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "But in an earthquake, the ground jerks back and forth, and the house jerks with it.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The top whips harder than the bottom, and the walls crack.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you put steel bars inside the concrete walls. Now they don't crack.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "But a stiff house passes every jolt to everything inside, and the furniture goes flying.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you cut the house off from the ground, and set it on thick pads of rubber and steel.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now the ground slides back and forth under it, and the house barely moves.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The pads are stiff up and down, so it doesn't bounce, but soft sideways.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "In Japan, thousands of buildings, even hospitals, already sit on pads like these.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Congratulations! You just built an earthquake proof house. What should we build next?",
   "pausa": 0.2
  }
 ],
